The stretch is inside one phrase (ち of こんにちは aligned to 23 s, は after it), so it can't be split between words, and _trim_long_words only shortens the long character, not the phrase's span. The start is kept rather than the end because most of these subtitles were followed directly by the next one (gap_after 0): the speech is at the start and the music after it. A reading time rather than a flat 7 s cap, since 7 s of こんにちは is still wrong; the speeds are the usual subtitle guidelines (about 15-17 characters a second for English, 4-5 for Japanese), with the existing no_spaces flag choosing between them rather than a per-language table.
